    "['vpdn-group [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel authentication', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel authentication', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent for the Cisco `vpdn-group 1` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel authentication\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel destination-address 10.11.11.1\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel local-name \"Device\"\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper configuration requires additional parameters that would be derived from the context of the full Cisco configuration. The above assumes:\\n1. The L2TP tunnel uses authentication (as implied by the Cisco config)\\n2. The destination address is the router\\'s interface address (10.11.11.1 from GE1/0/0)\\n3. The local name matches the Cisco hostname \"Device\"\\n\\nIn practice, you might need additional Juniper statements to fully replicate all the L2TP/VPDN functionality from the Cisco configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel authentication'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel destination-address 10.11.11.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel destination-address 10.11.11.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent for the Cisco `vpdn-group 1` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel authentication\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel destination-address 10.11.11.1\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel local-name \"Device\"\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper configuration requires additional parameters that would be derived from the context of the full Cisco configuration. The above assumes:\\n1. The L2TP tunnel uses authentication (as implied by the Cisco config)\\n2. The destination address is the router\\'s interface address (10.11.11.1 from GE1/0/0)\\n3. The local name matches the Cisco hostname \"Device\"\\n\\nIn practice, you might need additional Juniper statements to fully replicate all the L2TP/VPDN functionality from the Cisco configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel destination-address 10.11.11.1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel local-name \"Device\"', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel local-name \"Device\"', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent for the Cisco `vpdn-group 1` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel authentication\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel destination-address 10.11.11.1\\nset access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel local-name \"Device\"\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper configuration requires additional parameters that would be derived from the context of the full Cisco configuration. The above assumes:\\n1. The L2TP tunnel uses authentication (as implied by the Cisco config)\\n2. The destination address is the router\\'s interface address (10.11.11.1 from GE1/0/0)\\n3. The local name matches the Cisco hostname \"Device\"\\n\\nIn practice, you might need additional Juniper statements to fully replicate all the L2TP/VPDN functionality from the Cisco configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set access l2tp-access-profile \"1\" tunnel local-name \"Device\"'}]]": 1,
