    "['protocol [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols l2tp', 'parent_command': ['set services l2tp lns tunnel-group [parameter1]', 'interface [parameter2]'], 'root': 'set services l2tp lns tunnel-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"protocol l2tp\" command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols l2tp\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, this enables L2TP protocol globally. The full VPDN/L2TP configuration in Juniper would require additional context-specific commands to match Cisco\\'s complete L2TP termination functionality, including tunnel authentication and virtual interface binding.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols l2tp'}]]": 1,
    "['virtual-template [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces vt-1 unit 0 family inet', 'parent_command': ['set services l2tp lns tunnel-group [parameter1]', 'interface [parameter2]'], 'root': 'set services l2tp lns tunnel-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces vt-1 unit 0 family inet\\n##\\n\\nIn Juniper, Virtual-Template interfaces are equivalent to Virtual-Template (vt) interfaces. The command creates a virtual template interface (vt-1) with unit 0 and enables IPv4 family (inet) on it, which is the closest equivalent to Cisco's `virtual-template 1` command in the context of L2TP configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces vt-1 unit 0 family inet'}]]": 1,
    "['l2tp-class [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols l2tp lns LNS', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols l2tp lns LNS', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols l2tp lns LNS\\n##\\n\\nThis Juniper command creates an L2TP LNS (L2TP Network Server) configuration context named \"LNS\", which is equivalent to the Cisco \"l2tp-class LNS\" configuration. The actual L2TP parameters (like password, cookie settings, etc.) would then be configured under this context in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols l2tp lns LNS'}]]": 1,
    "['password [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set access profile LNS client l2tp password \"<password>\"', 'parent_command': ['set protocols l2tp lns LNS'], 'root': 'set protocols l2tp lns LNS', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the L2TP password setting:\\n\\n##\\nset access profile LNS client l2tp password \"<password>\"\\n##\\n\\nNote: This assumes you\\'re configuring an L2TP tunnel in Juniper with a profile named \"LNS\" (matching the l2tp-class name from the Cisco config). The exact context may vary slightly based on how the Juniper L2TP configuration is structured.', 'trans_template': 'set access profile LNS client l2tp password \"<password>\"'}]]": 1,
    "['hidden', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols l2tp tunnel password <password> secret', 'parent_command': ['set protocols l2tp lns LNS'], 'root': 'set protocols l2tp lns LNS', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'In Juniper OS, the equivalent command to Cisco\\'s `hidden` (which hides the L2TP password in the configuration) would be `secret` (as Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct \"hidden\" keyword but treats secrets as hidden by default). However, for L2TP configuration in Juniper, you\\'d typically set the password under the `lt2p` hierarchy.\\n\\nHere\\'s the translated Juniper command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols l2tp tunnel password <password> secret\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper automatically hides sensitive information like passwords in the configuration, so no separate \"hidden\" command is needed. The `secret` keyword in this context indicates the password should be encrypted/stored securely.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols l2tp tunnel password <password> secret'}]]": 1,
